meta-sysinfo
============

A Yocto layer that packages the `mcsysteminfo` system information utility.

The layer contains a single real recipe, `mcsysteminfo`, which is a worked
example of a CMake based application recipe: it fetches the sources from a
git repository, cross compiles them with CMake, splits the result into a
runtime and a development package, installs a systemd service and ships a
runtime test.

Table of Contents
=================

  I. Adding the meta-sysinfo layer to your build
 II. Building mcsysteminfo
III. What the recipe does
 IV. Using mcsysteminfo in an image
  V. Dependencies
 VI. Maintainer


I. Adding the meta-sysinfo layer to your build
==============================================

Add the layer to your build with:

    bitbake-layers add-layer /path/to/meta-sysinfo

or, if the layer is already cloned next to poky:

    bitbake-layers add-layer meta-sysinfo

To remove it again:

    bitbake-layers remove-layer meta-sysinfo


II. Building mcsysteminfo
=========================

Build the binary package on its own:

    bitbake mcsysteminfo

The resulting packages are written to:

    tmp/deploy/ipk/<machine>/mcsysteminfo_0.1.0_*.ipk
    tmp/deploy/ipk/<machine>/mcsysteminfo-dev_0.1.0_*.ipk

To pull the utility into an image, add it to the image recipe:

    IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " mcsysteminfo"

and rebuild the image.


III. What the recipe does
=========================

Sources
    The sources are fetched from
    https://github.com/KostiantynBushko/mcsysteminfo at a pinned commit
    (`SRCREV`). The version of the recipe and the version of the upstream
    project are kept in sync.

Build
    `inherit cmake` drives the cross build. `EXTRA_OECMAKE` turns the unit
    test on, `inherit pkgconfig` installs the `.pc` file.

Packaging
    `mcsysteminfo` contains the binary, the static library and the systemd
    unit. `mcsysteminfo-dev` contains the headers, the pkg-config file and
    the CMake package config.

Systemd
    `mcsysteminfo.service` is a oneshot service that writes a JSON inventory
    of the system to `/var/lib/mcsysteminfo/report.json`. This is the
    quickest way to find out which kernel, hardware and image a device
    actually booted.

    The service is installed but not started automatically, the
    `mcsysteminfo_0.1.0.bbappend` in this layer sets
    `SYSTEMD_AUTO_ENABLE:mcsysteminfo = "disable"`. A product layer that
    wants the inventory on every boot enables it with:

        SYSTEMD_AUTO_ENABLE:mcsysteminfo = "enable"

    The service can always be started by hand on the target:

        systemctl start mcsysteminfo.service

Tests
    The unit test is cross compiled together with the binary and installed
    into the `mcsysteminfo-ptest` package. It is executed on the target by
    the image runtime test framework, not on the build host. Add
    `ptest` to `IMAGE_FEATURES` to enable runtime testing.


IV. Using mcsysteminfo in an image
==================================

On the target:

    mcsysteminfo              # human readable report
    mcsysteminfo --json       # machine readable report
    cat /var/lib/mcsysteminfo/report.json


V. Dependencies
===============

  URI: https://git.yoctoproject.org/git/poky
  branch: scarthgap

  URI: https://github.com/KostiantynBushko/mcsysteminfo
  branch: main

The layer is compatible with the `scarthgap` series. Change
`LAYERSERIES_COMPAT_meta-sysinfo` in `conf/layer.conf` when moving to a
different release.

A `systemd` based image is required for the boot time inventory. On an image
without systemd, drop the `systemd` inheritance from the recipe.

A full build needs a few gigabytes of free disk space for the toolchain, the
build directory and the downloads.


VI. Maintainer
==============

Konstyantyn Bushko <kostiantyn.bushko@gmail.com>

Please submit patches to the meta-sysinfo layer to the maintainer.
